WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.830
- Thanks to Indochino for
keeping Legal Eagle in the air

00:00:02.830 --> 00:00:05.584
and helping me look fly.
(eagle caws)

00:00:05.584 --> 00:00:07.080
(bird caws loudly)
(Benton screams)

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:08.790
- Dr. Quest, a pleasure.

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:11.910
- Please, call me Benton. (chuckles)

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:14.610
And may I call you...
(typewriter keys clicking)

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:16.420
- Mr. Birdman.
- Mr. Birdman.

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:20.500
- Okay, is it Harvey Bird-man,
or is it Harvey Birdman?

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:22.320
I always thought it
was Bird-man growing up

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.070
like the superhero, but, upon reflection,

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:26.510
I thought it was a play on
words like a person's last name.

00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:28.200
It seems like they're using both here,

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:29.860
and I have no idea which one it is.

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:32.772
As you can tell, bird
law is not my specialty.

00:00:32.772 --> 00:00:36.610
(bright, plucky instrumental music)

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:39.160
Hey, Legal Eagles, it's
time think like a lawyer.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:40.610
And today, I am excited

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:43.480
to be covering Harvey
Birdman, Attorney at Law.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:46.340
Highly requested, and I used
to watch this all the time

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:48.300
as a kid, so I'm really excited to see

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:50.860
how this holds up as a practicing lawyer.

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:52.580
This is actually going to be the first

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:56.670
in a couple of cartoon trials
that I'm going to cover.

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:58.580
Let me know what you think
the next one is going to be

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:00.600
because I am really
excited about the next one;

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:02.790
it's one of my absolute favorites.

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:04.820
As always, be sure to comment in the form

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:07.850
of an objection, which I will
either sustain or overrule,

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:09.500
and stick around until
the end of the video,

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:13.310
where I give Harvey Birdman
a grade for legal realism.

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:16.170
So, without further ado, let's
dig into the first episode

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:18.864
of Harvey Birdman, Attorney at Law.

00:01:18.864 --> 00:01:22.350
(bright, jaunty instrumental music)

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:24.380
Okay, so the setup for this episode

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:28.780
is that the two father figures
in, I think it's Jonny Quest,

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:32.410
are having a dispute, and the person who

00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:35.070
is not Jonny Quest's father

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:38.030
is taking off with the two boys

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:41.530
and wants paternity of the two of them,

00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:45.540
much to the chagrin of
their scientist father.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:46.990
- I don't know, Benton.

00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:49.137
I guess I think of the boys as my own.

00:01:49.137 --> 00:01:49.970
(oven timer dings)

00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:51.460
- Of course you do, Race;

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:53.483
you're like a second father to them.

00:01:53.483 --> 00:01:54.903
- What if that's not good enough,

00:01:54.903 --> 00:01:57.310
(swallows loudly) being a second father?

00:01:57.310 --> 00:01:59.060
- I'm afraid I don't catch your meaning.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:01.290
- I want the boys, Benton, full time!

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:03.160
I've raised them, and
I think it's only fair!

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:04.630
- Race, you're tired.

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:07.040
The adventure of the yeti
snow princess took a lot

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:08.100
out of both of us.

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:09.571
Take a couple of days.

00:02:09.571 --> 00:02:10.960
(grunts loudly) Ow, ow, ow.
(bones crack)

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:12.160
- I'm taking the boys, Benton,

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:14.390
and there's not a damn
thing you can do about it!

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:16.390
- That's definitely assault and battery.

00:02:17.456 --> 00:02:18.800
(Benton whimpers)
- I forgot my keys.

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:19.633
- Oh.
(keys jingle)

00:02:19.633 --> 00:02:21.570
- Potential theft of a car.

00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:23.820
- Harvey Birdman, attorney at law,

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:27.095
dictating August 14th, 2:34 PM.

00:02:27.095 --> 00:02:30.094
Dictating...
(typewriter keys clicking)

00:02:30.094 --> 00:02:33.677
Hmm, dictating.
- Dictating, Harvey Birdman.

00:02:34.759 --> 00:02:38.425
(laughs)
(Harvey clicking tongue)

00:02:38.425 --> 00:02:39.830
(Harvey blows raspberries)

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:42.680
Okay, so this is actually hilarious

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:45.130
because it has a kernel of truth.

00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:47.570
Back in the day, really,
just a couple of years

00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:50.310
before I started practicing,
it was fairly common

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:52.910
for attorneys to take dictation.

00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:56.400
So they would record their
thoughts onto some sort

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:59.710
of reel-to-reel tape or a
little micro cassette or,

00:02:59.710 --> 00:03:02.150
if you were really fancy,
directly onto a computer.

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:05.560
And then you would have your
legal secretary transcribe all

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:08.000
of that, and, if they were a
really good legal secretary,

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.260
then they would turn that into the basis

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:13.740
for a pleading or a letter
to opposing counsel.

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:16.460
And this is exactly
what it would look like,

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:20.110
and often you would have
these large gaps in the tape

00:03:20.110 --> 00:03:23.350
where you were waiting
to think of what else

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:25.450
to say for this particular thing.

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:28.600
Sadly, using dictaphones
has gone out of favor,

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:30.030
and now we just type everything

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:32.290
'cause most lawyers, I
think, can type faster

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:33.440
than they can actually talk.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:36.410
So you really would not
save any time doing this,

00:03:36.410 --> 00:03:38.530
but this is totally
accurate to what it used

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:39.920
to be back in the day.

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:40.753
- It.

00:03:40.753 --> 00:03:42.480
- [Secretary] A Dr. Quest
to see you, Mr. Birdman.

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:44.091
- Send him in.

00:03:44.091 --> 00:03:45.443
(bird caws loudly)
(Benton screams)

00:03:45.443 --> 00:03:47.300
- Dr. Quest, a pleasure.

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:50.430
- Please, call me Benton. (chuckle)

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:53.410
And may I call you...
(typewriter keys clicking)

00:03:53.410 --> 00:03:55.220
- Mr. Birdman.
- Mr. Birdman.

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:59.310
- Okay, is it Harvey Bird-man,
or is it Harvey Birdman?

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:01.120
I always thought it was
Bird-man growing up,

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:02.880
like the superhero, but upon reflection,

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:05.300
I thought it was a play on
words, like a person's last name.

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:07.000
It seems like they're using both here,

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:08.660
and I have no idea which one it is.

00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:11.994
As you can tell, bird
law is not my specialty.

00:04:11.994 --> 00:04:14.110
- Bye bye birdie.

00:04:14.110 --> 00:04:16.100
- What makes Mr. Bannon
believe he has any claim

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:16.933
on your children?

00:04:16.933 --> 00:04:19.640
- Well, I hired him to
look after the boys, but,

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:22.950
as time went on, he became
more a second father to them.

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:23.837
I'd say I'm a good father.

00:04:23.837 --> 00:04:25.600
- Hello, Dr. Quest.
- Hey, Dad.

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:26.856
- But maybe I should've made more

00:04:26.856 --> 00:04:29.090
(explosion booms)
time for the boys.

00:04:29.090 --> 00:04:30.890
(liquid bubbling in beaker)
Hmm.

00:04:30.890 --> 00:04:33.750
- The cat's in the cradle
and the silver ball.

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:34.880
I'll take the case!

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:37.170
But first, I need to speak to the boys.

00:04:37.170 --> 00:04:38.003
What are their names?

00:04:38.003 --> 00:04:40.290
- Oh, of course, their names, certainly.

00:04:40.290 --> 00:04:43.100
Well, uh, yes, um, well
there's a blond one.

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:47.000
Um, the blond one, whose name is...

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:48.650
Oh dear.

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:50.536
Come on, Benton.

00:04:50.536 --> 00:04:51.369
(boom)
(briefcase latches click)

00:04:51.369 --> 00:04:52.210
- (chuckles) Oh God.

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:54.040
There are certain
procedures that you can do

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:56.920
to remove custody of children away

00:04:56.920 --> 00:04:58.510
from their biological parent.

00:04:58.510 --> 00:05:00.450
It's very, very difficult to do.

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:02.410
But you can imagine if the father

00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:06.480
did not actually know the
names of the children,

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:09.340
that would be probably
prima facie evidence

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:11.990
that he should not have
custody over these children

00:05:11.990 --> 00:05:14.730
or putting them in very
dangerous situations,

00:05:14.730 --> 00:05:16.090
like playing with explosives.

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:17.660
So (clicks tongue and laughs) yeah,

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:20.010
they're gonna have an
uphill battle on this one.

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:22.010
- One minute I'm a penniless orphan stuck

00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:23.230
in a street-sweeping caste.

00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:25.280
The next, I'm on this
really cool needly plane

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:27.780
that goes very, very fast,
drinking cold American beverages!

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:29.527
I owe Dr. Quest very much big time.

00:05:29.527 --> 00:05:30.530
- And you?

00:05:30.530 --> 00:05:32.496
- Hey, you know, he's my dad.

00:05:32.496 --> 00:05:33.429
(intercom beeps)
- Yes.

00:05:33.429 --> 00:05:35.070
- [Secretary] That was the ornithologist.

00:05:35.070 --> 00:05:36.190
The results were negative.

00:05:36.190 --> 00:05:37.350
- Thank you.

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:38.260
- So this is really good.

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:40.900
Harvey Bird-man, or
Birdman, whichever it is

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:43.380
in this particular episode,
is doing a good job

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:45.600
of interviewing the prospective witnesses.

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:47.890
That would be very important
when you're dealing

00:05:47.890 --> 00:05:50.790
with a custody suit like this,

00:05:50.790 --> 00:05:52.650
and it would be important for any trial.

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:54.900
So you actually really
don't see witness prep going

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:58.550
on very often in dramas,
so good job to this cartoon

00:05:58.550 --> 00:06:00.800
for actually getting
one of the mundane parts

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:02.904
of legal practice correctly.

00:06:02.904 --> 00:06:06.790
(doors squeak open)
(tense music)

00:06:06.790 --> 00:06:08.101
- Oh, man.

00:06:08.101 --> 00:06:09.020
- Birdman!

00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:10.860
At last, we meet again.

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:12.520
- Yeah, I think it probably
goes without saying that,

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:13.740
obviously, you can't walk

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:16.184
into court wearing a bird suit and a mask.

00:06:16.184 --> 00:06:17.800
- [Whispering Voice] Indochino.

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:20.040
- And fun fact, wearing a mask in public

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:22.820
is considered loitering
under New York law.

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:24.310
- Vulturo, it was last week.

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:26.530
- I was in Zurich last week, you nit!

00:06:26.530 --> 00:06:29.343
- Avenger, my personal digital assistant.

00:06:30.460 --> 00:06:33.740
- Um, how do you make a T again?

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:36.440
Do you push that and then
leave it down, or do, I--

00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:38.400
- I said I'd live on to defeat you,

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:40.300
and that happens this very day!

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:42.940
- Get ready to feel the power
(sharp whooshes)

00:06:42.940 --> 00:06:44.613
of attorney!

00:06:44.613 --> 00:06:46.598
(birds cawing)
(laughs)

00:06:46.598 --> 00:06:49.036
(laser noises)

00:06:49.036 --> 00:06:50.216
(judge bangs weapon on desk)
- Order, order

00:06:50.216 --> 00:06:51.520
in the courtroom!

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:53.260
- Oh my God, the power of attorney.

00:06:53.260 --> 00:06:55.150
The power of attorney!
(pulsating electronic noises)

00:06:55.150 --> 00:06:57.800
So, superpowers notwithstanding,

00:06:57.800 --> 00:07:01.150
it's actually pretty common for attorneys

00:07:01.150 --> 00:07:03.720
in specific niches like family law

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:05.490
to know each other very, very well.

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:06.480
Even in big cities,

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:10.030
there aren't that many
well-known family law attorneys,

00:07:10.030 --> 00:07:13.740
so if you dabble in that
area with some frequency,

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:15.980
you're gonna get to know everyone else

00:07:17.483 --> 00:07:18.550
in the family law bar.

00:07:18.550 --> 00:07:21.480
And so it makes total
sense that Harvey Birdman

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:23.970
knows this other person,
even if he, I think,

00:07:23.970 --> 00:07:25.830
in the background, is a
super villain as well.

00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:28.490
And also, true story,
when you pass the bar,

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:30.080
you gain the power of attorney,

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:32.230
which allows you to shoot electricity

00:07:32.230 --> 00:07:35.131
and energy out of your
briefcase and hands.

00:07:35.131 --> 00:07:37.013
(pulsating electronic noises)
The power of attorney!

00:07:38.008 --> 00:07:39.610
- You should not do that
in front of the judge.

00:07:39.610 --> 00:07:43.530
- Because someone is the
genetic parent of the child,

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:48.100
does that make him one most
fit to raise said child?

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:49.163
Ladies and gentlemen and the judge,

00:07:49.163 --> 00:07:53.252
(muttering rapidly and
incoherently) Quest family

00:07:53.252 --> 00:07:57.669
(muttering rapidly and incoherently)

00:08:00.050 --> 00:08:04.100
parents of these two adorable young boys.

00:08:04.100 --> 00:08:06.180
- Okay, so a couple of
things going on here.

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:08.510
Obviously, the attorney
should not be that close

00:08:08.510 --> 00:08:09.343
to the jury.

00:08:09.343 --> 00:08:11.360
You can't enter the
well without permission,

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:13.470
or the bailiff will tackle you,

00:08:13.470 --> 00:08:15.060
especially in a court surrounded

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:17.000
by super villains and superheroes.

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:18.770
Other than that, though, (chuckles)

00:08:18.770 --> 00:08:22.370
I will say that sometimes,
the attorneys' accents

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:25.530
can make it very, very
difficult to understand them.

00:08:25.530 --> 00:08:27.580
And I will say that when I was practicing

00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:33.320
in a very, very rural part of Virginia,

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:36.260
there were times when
I could not understand

00:08:36.260 --> 00:08:38.240
what the opposing attorney was saying.

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:39.073
So

00:08:40.580 --> 00:08:43.760
sometimes that happens, and
the attorney might not know

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:45.510
that he or she is losing the jury

00:08:45.510 --> 00:08:47.750
because what he or she is saying

00:08:47.750 --> 00:08:50.070
is totally incomprehensible.

00:08:50.070 --> 00:08:51.600
That definitely happens.

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:54.260
- State your first name, your
last name, and occupation.

00:08:54.260 --> 00:08:57.749
- Lizard Man, Lizard Man, and Lizard Man.

00:08:57.749 --> 00:09:00.230
- (mutters incoherently) witness.

00:09:00.230 --> 00:09:01.560
- Uh, sure.

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:04.630
From what I saw during
Mystery of the Lizard Men.

00:09:04.630 --> 00:09:05.492
It's Mystery Lizard Men, right?

00:09:05.492 --> 00:09:06.610
(suits squeaking)

00:09:06.610 --> 00:09:07.443
Right.

00:09:07.443 --> 00:09:09.460
Yeah, from what I saw, it was Mr. Bannon

00:09:09.460 --> 00:09:11.164
who was with the children the most.

00:09:11.164 --> 00:09:11.997
- Thank you.

00:09:11.997 --> 00:09:12.830
- Hey, good going, Bill.

00:09:12.830 --> 00:09:13.820
(men clapping)
- Nice job, Bill.

00:09:13.820 --> 00:09:14.653
- Next witness.

00:09:14.653 --> 00:09:15.755
- Now would you mind telling us

00:09:15.755 --> 00:09:17.670
in your opinion who the best parent is?

00:09:17.670 --> 00:09:20.570
- Sure, Quest was there physically.

00:09:20.570 --> 00:09:22.510
- Ah, but it was clear to me that Bannon

00:09:22.510 --> 00:09:24.080
was the primary caregiver.

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:25.560
- Best parent?

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:26.434
- Bannon!

00:09:26.434 --> 00:09:28.322
- (mutters incoherently)

00:09:28.322 --> 00:09:29.490
- Bannon.

00:09:29.490 --> 00:09:31.680
- Now normally, this kind of parade

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:36.560
of ridiculous witnesses is
totally, totally illegitimate.

00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:39.460
See, for example, the Seinfeld trial,

00:09:39.460 --> 00:09:40.910
where they just bring in character witness

00:09:40.910 --> 00:09:43.140
after character witness;
totally ridiculous.

00:09:43.140 --> 00:09:44.150
- [Man] He was a communist.

00:09:44.150 --> 00:09:44.983
- No.

00:09:44.983 --> 00:09:45.900
- She exposed her nipple.

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:46.733
- Mm-mm.

00:09:46.733 --> 00:09:48.840
But here, these witnesses actually

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:51.610
are not testifying to the character per se

00:09:51.610 --> 00:09:56.480
but to the parenting ability
of the two adults in question.

00:09:56.480 --> 00:09:58.850
I think all this testimony
probably would come in

00:09:58.850 --> 00:10:01.180
and is probative, in other words relevant,

00:10:01.180 --> 00:10:05.040
to the crucial question
of whether the children

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:06.780
are in danger and whether custody

00:10:06.780 --> 00:10:08.010
should be given over to the other person.

00:10:08.010 --> 00:10:10.700
So there should be some
cross examination going on.

00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:13.690
Harvey Birdman should be up and
cross examining these people

00:10:13.690 --> 00:10:16.818
but, all in all, the regular
testimony's pretty good.

00:10:16.818 --> 00:10:17.651
(siren beeping in distance)

00:10:17.651 --> 00:10:19.240
- Boy, that went well today.

00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:21.453
I'd say a celebration's in order.

00:10:21.453 --> 00:10:24.050
- Scotch, scotch, scotch, scotch.

00:10:24.050 --> 00:10:26.620
- Stan, they're 11.

00:10:26.620 --> 00:10:28.098
- Right.

00:10:28.098 --> 00:10:29.360
(plunk, plunk)

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:32.630
- Okay, of course it's
ridiculous to have the children

00:10:32.630 --> 00:10:35.160
in a bar, although that
does feed into the idea

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:38.140
that their father is not a good caretaker.

00:10:38.140 --> 00:10:41.450
I will say that after a day of trial,

00:10:41.450 --> 00:10:45.000
the place you are most likely
to find a lawyer is at a bar,

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:47.500
probably the one that's
closest to the courthouse.

00:10:49.090 --> 00:10:50.780
Yeah. (laughs)

00:10:50.780 --> 00:10:52.010
- You're up, Mr. Buttermilk.

00:10:52.010 --> 00:10:56.617
- I'd like to call to the
stand Dr. Benton Quest.

00:10:56.617 --> 00:10:58.550
(people murmuring)

00:10:58.550 --> 00:11:01.710
Dr. Quest, tell us a little
about your relationship with--

00:11:01.710 --> 00:11:02.543
- Well--

00:11:02.543 --> 00:11:05.100
- Way too close, way too
close to the witness.

00:11:05.100 --> 00:11:07.480
- Race and I met one night.

00:11:07.480 --> 00:11:08.694
- With your boys.

00:11:08.694 --> 00:11:11.110
- Oh, (laughs) right.

00:11:11.110 --> 00:11:12.460
I treasure them.

00:11:12.460 --> 00:11:14.023
I love them dearly.

00:11:15.755 --> 00:11:17.601
(hums)

00:11:17.601 --> 00:11:20.270
- (chuckles) That's technically improper.

00:11:20.270 --> 00:11:22.240
You're not allowed to
lead the witness that way,

00:11:22.240 --> 00:11:25.040
and it's really damning for his case.

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:27.500
- And when I'm not working on my projects,

00:11:27.500 --> 00:11:29.140
like a way of accelerating particles

00:11:29.140 --> 00:11:32.258
to subsonic speed using
just magnets, for instance,

00:11:32.258 --> 00:11:34.230
- Thank you, Dr. Quest.
- to create aberrent waveforms

00:11:34.230 --> 00:11:35.530
constant rate,
- Nothing further!

00:11:35.530 --> 00:11:38.250
- resulting in the sum which
surprisingly equals the mass

00:11:38.250 --> 00:11:39.510
of the atom.
(Harvey clearing throat)

00:11:39.510 --> 00:11:42.411
And separating them by
weight, we should see

00:11:42.411 --> 00:11:43.244
(harp plays)
(dove wings flap)

00:11:43.244 --> 00:11:44.860
magic!
(laughs)

00:11:44.860 --> 00:11:47.340
- Sometimes, it is so
hard to control a witness,

00:11:47.340 --> 00:11:49.010
and they think they're doing a good job,

00:11:49.010 --> 00:11:51.690
or they just get lost in whatever point

00:11:51.690 --> 00:11:52.523
that they're trying to make,

00:11:52.523 --> 00:11:54.803
whether it's good for the trial or not.

00:11:54.803 --> 00:11:57.240
(sighs) Yeah, I feel
for Harvey Birdman here.

00:11:57.240 --> 00:11:59.428
It's hard to control a witness sometimes.

00:11:59.428 --> 00:12:01.696
- Mr. Vulturo, your witness.

00:12:01.696 --> 00:12:05.680
- (mutters incoherently) spend
enough time with your boys?

00:12:05.680 --> 00:12:07.744
- Why, yes, I think I have.

00:12:07.744 --> 00:12:10.190
- (mutters incoherently) doing what?

00:12:10.190 --> 00:12:12.290
- Things that a father does with his sons.

00:12:13.380 --> 00:12:15.500
- (mutters incoherently) would include?

00:12:15.500 --> 00:12:16.333
- Well--

00:12:16.333 --> 00:12:18.340
- Hmm, would you mind telling me

00:12:18.340 --> 00:12:22.460
if Jonny and Hadji know
how to play baseball?

00:12:22.460 --> 00:12:25.420
- Why, uh, I'm not sure.

00:12:25.420 --> 00:12:27.190
- So here

00:12:27.190 --> 00:12:29.900
you might think that the policy

00:12:29.900 --> 00:12:32.040
of never asking a question
of cross examination

00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:35.110
that you don't already know
the answer to is sacrosanct.

00:12:35.110 --> 00:12:36.700
But this is actually a good example

00:12:36.700 --> 00:12:39.720
of when you should deviate from that rule

00:12:39.720 --> 00:12:41.640
because there's really no answer

00:12:41.640 --> 00:12:45.820
that Dr. Quest can give
here that will be good.

00:12:45.820 --> 00:12:48.290
Obviously, the attorney
knows that this guy

00:12:48.290 --> 00:12:50.020
doesn't know his children very well,

00:12:50.020 --> 00:12:53.830
and if he answers the
question correctly, well,

00:12:53.830 --> 00:12:55.670
that he knows whether his children know

00:12:55.670 --> 00:12:57.330
how to play baseball or not, well,

00:12:57.330 --> 00:12:59.120
he can just move on to the next question

00:12:59.120 --> 00:13:01.200
without really any damage being done.

00:13:01.200 --> 00:13:05.110
However, if the doctor
fails to answer the question

00:13:05.110 --> 00:13:08.040
in the way that he is
clearly failing to do here,

00:13:08.040 --> 00:13:09.710
then he will have played into the hands,

00:13:09.710 --> 00:13:14.060
and that testimony will be
devastating to Dr. Quest's case.

00:13:14.060 --> 00:13:17.500
So this is actually a good
example of asking a question

00:13:17.500 --> 00:13:19.410
that you might not
necessarily know the answer to

00:13:19.410 --> 00:13:22.080
ahead of time, but it
can lead to some really,

00:13:22.080 --> 00:13:24.710
really good and damning testimony.

00:13:24.710 --> 00:13:25.930
- Look, I don't know

00:13:25.930 --> 00:13:29.010
why basesball means so much to everyone.

00:13:29.010 --> 00:13:31.030
There's important work to be done

00:13:31.030 --> 00:13:33.000
to make things more decent!

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:36.000
- More decent than baseball?

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:36.833
Dun dun dun!

00:13:37.803 --> 00:13:39.940
- (chuckles) That would be argumentative.

00:13:39.940 --> 00:13:42.470
- [Vulturo] How long
have you known the boys?

00:13:42.470 --> 00:13:45.660
- Why, four years,
almost half their lives.

00:13:45.660 --> 00:13:48.780
- And how would you describe
your relationship with them?

00:13:48.780 --> 00:13:50.920
- I'm their bodyguard and tutor.

00:13:50.920 --> 00:13:53.060
- This would also be improper
because this attorney

00:13:53.060 --> 00:13:55.280
has already called all of his witnesses

00:13:55.280 --> 00:13:58.560
and then turned it over to the Quests,

00:13:58.560 --> 00:14:00.500
presumably the respondent
or the defendant.

00:14:00.500 --> 00:14:02.950
So you don't then get
to call new witnesses.

00:14:02.950 --> 00:14:04.860
You'd be allowed to
call rebuttal witnesses,

00:14:04.860 --> 00:14:06.380
but this is your main witness,

00:14:06.380 --> 00:14:07.870
so he's not a rebuttal witness.

00:14:07.870 --> 00:14:10.750
So the order of the
witnesses is totally wrong.

00:14:10.750 --> 00:14:12.600
But you see this in a lot of legal shows

00:14:12.600 --> 00:14:15.020
where they call witnesses out of order

00:14:15.020 --> 00:14:16.433
to make it more dramatic.

00:14:18.050 --> 00:14:20.010
Harvey Birdman is no exception.

00:14:20.010 --> 00:14:22.670
- This doesn't seem like Race.

00:14:22.670 --> 00:14:23.503
- Hmm?

00:14:23.503 --> 00:14:26.510
- [Vulturo] Well, what
do the boys mean to you?

00:14:26.510 --> 00:14:29.300
- [Race] They mean, they
mean everything to me.

00:14:29.300 --> 00:14:30.133
- Excuse me.
- I've taught them

00:14:30.133 --> 00:14:31.500
how to drive a hover boat,

00:14:31.500 --> 00:14:33.580
- Shh.
- how to tie a tourniquet,

00:14:33.580 --> 00:14:35.590
but they've taught me so much more.

00:14:35.590 --> 00:14:36.847
They've taught me to care,

00:14:36.847 --> 00:14:38.150
(poignant music)
to nurture,

00:14:38.150 --> 00:14:39.900
and I don't see why a simple thing

00:14:39.900 --> 00:14:41.470
like biology should get in the way

00:14:41.470 --> 00:14:43.838
of what I know in my heart to be true.

00:14:43.838 --> 00:14:45.500
- Thank you.

00:14:45.500 --> 00:14:46.650
Your witness.

00:14:46.650 --> 00:14:49.020
- That was really good direct testimony

00:14:49.020 --> 00:14:50.540
and really good open-ended questions

00:14:50.540 --> 00:14:53.050
from the lawyer, eliciting
a great narrative

00:14:53.050 --> 00:14:54.900
and some great sound
bytes from the witness.

00:14:54.900 --> 00:14:57.200
So great job with direct examination.

00:14:57.200 --> 00:14:58.630
- Bootylicious!

00:14:58.630 --> 00:14:59.530
- Yes, your Honor?

00:14:59.530 --> 00:15:00.520
- Your witness.

00:15:00.520 --> 00:15:01.890
- Yes, your Honor.

00:15:01.890 --> 00:15:04.330
Mr. Bannon, how long
have you known the boys?

00:15:04.330 --> 00:15:06.536
- Why, four years, almost half their--

00:15:06.536 --> 00:15:07.730
- Fascinating.

00:15:07.730 --> 00:15:12.730
Tell me, Mr. Bannon, what do
you have to say about this?

00:15:13.620 --> 00:15:15.150
Sorry.

00:15:15.150 --> 00:15:16.340
- Yeah, you're not allowed in the well.

00:15:16.340 --> 00:15:17.360
- Hm, let's see.

00:15:17.360 --> 00:15:19.180
No, I tried that one already.

00:15:19.180 --> 00:15:21.399
What do you have to say about this?

00:15:21.399 --> 00:15:22.735
(electric zap)
(cries out)

00:15:22.735 --> 00:15:23.568
(sizzle)

00:15:23.568 --> 00:15:24.740
- Order, order!
(weapon bangs against desk)

00:15:24.740 --> 00:15:28.230
Mr. Birdman, you better
have a damn good explanation

00:15:28.230 --> 00:15:29.870
for this.
- I do, your Honor.

00:15:29.870 --> 00:15:30.703
A few months ago,

00:15:30.703 --> 00:15:32.690
during the Adventure of the
Sunken Safe Deposit Box,

00:15:32.690 --> 00:15:34.330
Race mysteriously became left handed,

00:15:34.330 --> 00:15:36.010
and his shoes suddenly had laces when,

00:15:36.010 --> 00:15:37.620
up till then, they'd always been slip-ons.

00:15:37.620 --> 00:15:38.453
See, the bartender

00:15:38.453 --> 00:15:39.970
that gave Race a drugged
frozen lime daiquiri

00:15:39.970 --> 00:15:41.280
during the House of Tasteful Men

00:15:41.280 --> 00:15:43.600
was the same man who, Judge,
later dinged your Lexus

00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:46.000
and drove off without exchanging
insurance information.

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:47.750
This man created a robot Race Bannon

00:15:47.750 --> 00:15:49.330
and programmed it to get the boys.

00:15:49.330 --> 00:15:51.730
And that man, your Honor,

00:15:51.730 --> 00:15:54.380
is in this very

00:15:54.380 --> 00:15:55.610
courtroom.

00:15:55.610 --> 00:15:57.990
- Okay, putting aside the theatrics

00:15:57.990 --> 00:16:00.870
of a robot taking the
stand, that information

00:16:00.870 --> 00:16:04.040
should have come out in discovery
and not in trial itself.

00:16:04.040 --> 00:16:06.070
So if Harvey Birdman was a good lawyer,

00:16:06.070 --> 00:16:08.690
he would've discovered all
of this information months

00:16:08.690 --> 00:16:11.893
ahead of the trial itself;
that's what litigation is for.

00:16:12.780 --> 00:16:14.550
- Stenographer, read
back what I just said.

00:16:14.550 --> 00:16:18.810
- [Stenographer] Yes, um,
something about a Lexus and

00:16:19.650 --> 00:16:22.457
slip-on shoes and...

00:16:22.457 --> 00:16:23.610
- And that is totally accurate,

00:16:23.610 --> 00:16:25.520
that he could ask the stenographer,

00:16:25.520 --> 00:16:27.570
the court reporter, to read back part

00:16:27.570 --> 00:16:31.020
of the transcript in those
particular circumstances.

00:16:31.020 --> 00:16:31.853
That's really funny.

00:16:31.853 --> 00:16:33.380
- Oh, drat!

00:16:33.380 --> 00:16:37.460
This confounded machine
doesn't make any sense!

00:16:37.460 --> 00:16:38.870
- Doctor,

00:16:38.870 --> 00:16:40.020
Doctor--

00:16:40.020 --> 00:16:41.140
- Zin.

00:16:41.140 --> 00:16:43.565
- Why was Dr. Zin the
stenographer, though?

00:16:43.565 --> 00:16:45.033
- Quickly to the Vulturecraft!

00:16:46.730 --> 00:16:49.100
- If the lawyer was complicit

00:16:49.100 --> 00:16:52.120
with the super villain's attempts
to get custody of the boys

00:16:52.120 --> 00:16:53.480
and suborned

00:16:54.420 --> 00:16:58.150
perjury and this false witness,

00:16:58.150 --> 00:17:00.140
that guy could get disbarred.

00:17:00.140 --> 00:17:01.150
God, it's so good!

00:17:01.150 --> 00:17:02.240
I love Harvey Birdman.

00:17:02.240 --> 00:17:03.480
I hope you guys like this 'cause I'd love

00:17:03.480 --> 00:17:04.806
to do another episode of this.

00:17:04.806 --> 00:17:08.760
(bright, jaunty instrumental music)

00:17:08.760 --> 00:17:12.620
It is time to give Harvey
Birdman, Attorney at Law a grade

00:17:12.620 --> 00:17:13.894
for legal realism.

00:17:13.894 --> 00:17:16.380
(gavel bangs repeatedly)

00:17:16.380 --> 00:17:20.110
So on the one hand, you have
some pretty good trial tactics:

00:17:20.110 --> 00:17:21.630
good direct examination,

00:17:21.630 --> 00:17:23.680
reasonable cross examination tactics,

00:17:23.680 --> 00:17:26.880
and some testimony that
was actually relevant

00:17:26.880 --> 00:17:28.710
to the question at hand,

00:17:28.710 --> 00:17:30.630
which you actually don't
see all that often.

00:17:30.630 --> 00:17:33.960
On the other hand, you have
an abbreviated time frame

00:17:33.960 --> 00:17:36.900
that is totally unrealistic,
you've got superheroes

00:17:36.900 --> 00:17:39.330
and superpowers going
off in the courtroom,

00:17:39.330 --> 00:17:44.330
and some totally ridiculous
nonsense with some robots

00:17:44.390 --> 00:17:45.470
and testimony.

00:17:45.470 --> 00:17:47.020
But all in all, I have to say this is one

00:17:47.020 --> 00:17:49.580
of the more accurate cartoons
when you give it a discount

00:17:49.580 --> 00:17:51.860
for trying to be a
comedy and a kid's show.

00:17:51.860 --> 00:17:55.670
So I will give Harvey Birdman,
Attorney at Law a B plus

00:17:55.670 --> 00:17:56.820
for legal accuracy.

00:17:56.820 --> 00:17:59.210
You can really feel the power of attorney.

00:17:59.210 --> 00:18:01.070
